# Rank 1150 / CVE-2026-25083: GROWI's OpenAI thread and message routes look
# up a ThreadRelation by threadId alone, so a logged-in user who learned a
# shared AI assistant's thread id could read or post into another user's
# thread (CWE-862). postMessageHandlersFactory and its siblings
# (get-messages, get-threads, delete-thread, edit) call
#
#   const threadRelation = await ThreadRelationModel.findOne({ threadId });
#
# with threadId taken straight from req.body/req.params and no filter tying
# the row to req.user. The fixed revision adds userId: user._id to every
# ThreadRelationModel query and to getThreadsByAiAssistantId's and
# deleteThread's signatures.
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary gap, re-confirmed on a second pass with two measured
# candidate bindings. Scanning either revision reports nothing on the
# construct: req.body is labelled code.HttpInput at the threadId destructure
# but ThreadRelationModel.findOne carries no sink or check label in either
# revision, and the full findings view over the whole apps/app tree (583
# findings) contains no authorization finding in the openai feature at all.
# code.IdorPolicyReview (VYQL-AUTH-081) is the right concept and its
# JavaScript binding is a per-project presence match on prisma.accounts.update.
#
# The second pass measured the two forms that shape could take, on this
# checkout, three --cache off runs per revision over apps/app:
#
#   * call-scoped -- a Mongoose model read (findOne/findById/find and the
#     six write siblings, dependency("mongoose") gated) whose filter
#     argument carries no conventional ownership key (userId, user, owner,
#     createdBy, accountId, tenantId, memberId). 1053 VYQL-AUTH-081 rows on
#     the vulnerable tree and 1053 on the fixed one: the four fixed call
#     sites are 4 rows of 1053, and the count does not move.
#   * handler-scoped -- the same model read inside a function that mentions
#     the principal (identifier contains "user"), negated on the function's
#     prop tokens instead of the call's argument. 353 rows on vulnerable,
#     351 on fixed, from a baseline of zero. It reaches one of the four
#     fixed sites (post-message); the other three keep their query in the
#     service layer, where the vulnerable function names no principal at
#     all, so the conjunct meant to buy precision is what drops them.
#
# The third test below is why neither form can be shipped: updateThreads is
# the same project's own maintenance path, an ownerless find over every
# knowledge thread of an assistant, and it is correct code. A lookup that
# legitimately owns every row it returns has the identical shape to the
# IDOR, so any binding narrow enough to skip it has to name this project's
# field names -- the same flat-bag negation declined at ranks 1074, 1081 and
# 1311. No definition change shipped.
#
# One engine fact was isolated while measuring the handler-scoped form, with
# a two-file pair that differs only in the property's spelling: a shorthand
# property ({ threadId }) emits no prop: token at all, while the same
# property written with a value ({ threadId: threadId }) does
# (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go, case "pair"). The
# handler-scoped form therefore discriminates on this project's fixed
# revision partly because the fix writes userId: user._id with an explicit
# value; a project writing the same fix as { threadId, userId } would still
# be reported. Filed as an engine gap rather than worked around.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

